From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 20:22:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] dndcp: Wayland drag and drop

Drag and drop is delegated to the existing X11 implementation, so under a
Wayland session the detection window is an Xwayland client and the compositor
bridges its XDND to native clients. Three things stopped that working, each
of which alone was enough to lose every guest to host drag.

The detection window set override-redirect. A compositor bridging a native
Wayland drag to an Xwayland window looks its target up among managed clients,
so an unmanaged window is never offered the drag and no XdndEnter arrives. On
KWin that lookup is Workspace::findClient() by way of
XwlDropHandler::updateDragTarget(). Keep the window managed and suppress
focus with _NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE_DND, _NET_WM_USER_TIME, skip taskbar, skip
pager and WM_HINTS input False instead.

This is not particular to KWin. On sway 1.11 a native Wayland drag offering
text/uri-list reaches an XdndAware Xwayland window and completes the
handshake; the same window created override-redirect is offered nothing, the
drag beginning and ending without a single XdndEnter.

A window does not become a drag target the moment it maps. The compositor has
to adopt it and it has to paint, both of which happen roughly 100ms after
MapNotify, and nothing re-evaluates the choice afterwards: the target is
picked on pointer motion, and the only motion in this sequence is the warp
that follows immediately. Wait for the window to appear in _NET_CLIENT_LIST
first. The code here previously slept 300 microseconds.

That wait does not iterate the main loop. It runs from an RPC handler, and
GuestDnDMgr::OnRpcQueryExiting() shows the detection window before recording
the session id, so a command dispatched from inside the wait would run that
sequence again and the outer call would write the older session id last.
Every later command for the real session is rejected after that, which leaves
drag and drop broken until something resets it. Polling is enough: the window
manager acts on X requests that have already been flushed.

The detection window is shown twice in a host to guest drop, once to be
positioned and once to source the drag, and only the first of those may move
the pointer. Wiggling it again once the drag has started takes it off the
target the host chose, moments before the button is released: the drag ends
in GDK_DRAG_CANCEL_NO_TARGET and the file reaches the staging directory
without reaching the application it was dropped on. The two cases are told
apart by an explicit role rather than by inspecting drag state.

GTK's drop target preload picks application/vnd.portal.filetransfer when the
source is a KDE application, and the XDG document portal authenticates a
caller by opening /proc/<pid>/root. It cannot do that for a process exec'd by
a setuid wrapper, because that clears the kernel's dumpable flag and leaves
/proc/<pid> owned by root even after privileges are dropped. Read
text/uri-list directly instead, draining the stream asynchronously as
gdk_drop_read_finish() requires.

Also sync the X pointer after a uinput move. uinput moves the compositor's
pointer, and under Xwayland an X client learns the real position only while
the pointer is over one of its own surfaces, so XDND would otherwise pick its
target from a stale position.

With drag and drop in place the backend reports the same capabilities as the
X11 one, DND_CP_CAP_FORMATS_ALL.

Note that dragDetWndX11GTK4.cpp is shared with the X11 backend, so the
managed detection window, the adoption wait and the explicit uri-list read
apply to plain X11 GTK4 sessions as well.

Tested on Fedora 44 with KDE Plasma 6.7.5 on Wayland, against VMware
Workstation Pro 26H1u1 (26.0.1.25688693) on Windows 10 Pro 22H2: a 16MB zip
dragged from Dolphin lands on the host, and the handshake completes in about
110ms.
